{"id":"facilities-fund-recipients","title":"The facilities fund: who the money was passed to","question":"The one pass-through agency that names its recipients is the facilities fund. Who are the largest, are any privately owned, and do any share an officer with the fund?","finding":"The City paid the Low Income Investment Fund $79.9 million in fiscal 2021 to 2025 for child-care buildings. Its tax returns for those years name 39 San Francisco centre operators and City-paid nonprofits receiving $48.4 million, led by Wu Yee Children's Services with $8.9 million, itself one of the two referral agencies. 5 of the named recipients ($1.2 million) are private companies, individual licensees or corporations whose tax status was not found. A further 57 San Francisco recipients received $5.0 million; they include home-based providers and people and are not listed. Together that is $53.5 million of grants to San Francisco organizations against the $79.9 million the City paid in. 1 recipient shares an officer's name with the fund: Felton Institute, granted $7.9 million. Name match: the fund's returns for 2024–2025 list Yohana Quiroz as director; this organization's returns for 2021–2025 list the same name as chief operating officer. The fund's grants to it in the years the name is on the fund's board come to $3.5 million.","how":"Recipients are the grants to organizations listed on the fund's Form 990, Schedule I, for tax years ending June 2021 to June 2025, kept where the address is in San Francisco or the same tax number appears with a San Francisco address. A recipient is named here only when its name matches the licensee or name of a licensed child-care centre, or a City-paid nonprofit; everything else is added up and not listed. Licences, places, City network sites and State contracts are the recipient's own, from the other screens. Officers were compared by full name between the fund's returns and each recipient's returns, and a match is shown only where the name appears at no more than two organizations in the whole file and the two are also linked by a grant.","limits":"The fund also receives money from other cities, foundations and the federal government, so not every San Francisco grant is City money, and tax years do not line up exactly with the City's payments. The difference between what the City paid in and the San Francisco grants on the returns can be money held for building projects that take several years, grants to individual providers (reported without a place), and the fund's own costs of running the programme; the returns do not say. The legal form the fund records for a recipient is unreliable (it marks several charities as corporations), so kind of operator is taken from the licence and tax records instead. A building grant can go to a charity or a business; the fund's returns show grants to both. A shared officer is a name match between two returns; the fund answered no, in every year, to the return's question on grants to related persons, and its returns describe a conflict-of-interest policy with yearly disclosures by directors; whether the director took part in any decision is not in a public file. A recipient with no places listed may be licensed under another name. The payment file labels each line with a fiscal year that is not always the year the payment was issued: some earlier payments, mostly on purchase orders in multi-year capital funds, are filed under a later year, and the Controller's own published figures follow that label. Payments are dated here by voucher number, a single running sequence, which moves $5.52 billion of $119.3 billion (4.6%) to an earlier year than its label; $1.08 billion of that belongs to fiscal 2018 or earlier, before the file starts. Near the turn of a year the number dates a payment to within a few days.","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"recipient","t":"Recipient","type":"org"},{"k":"asNamed","t":"As named on the fund's return","type":"text"},{"k":"kind","t":"Kind of operat